---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/implementation-plan";
const title = "Shopify Email Implementation Plan \u2014 Release Gates";
const desc = "Build a Shopify email program with dependencies, acceptance tests, owners and rollback decisions.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How long should implementation take?",
    "answer": "Estimate from unresolved dependencies, staffing and the longest acceptance case. Stage dates are project estimates, not vendor performance claims."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which flows should go live first?",
    "answer": "Start with the highest-priority customer problem whose data and ownership are ready. Keep existing order notifications covered and add flows only when their exclusion rules can be demonstrated."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens when a release fails?",
    "answer": "Pause the affected new dispatch path, preserve queued and sent-message records, fix the cause and approve a controlled restart. Decide how missed messages are handled before replaying events."
  },
  {
    "question": "What proves completion?",
    "answer": "Signed functional evidence, a limited release review and an operational handoff. Commercial lift requires a separate measurement design; implementation alone does not prove it."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Start with a release record, not a deadline</h2>
    <p>This plan is for the retention lead commissioning new automation. Choose the first flow from the actual customer problem: missing product education, unhandled checkout intent, or a repeat-purchase opportunity. Do not enable a standard bundle merely because templates exist. Each release record needs a trigger, audience, exclusions, message purpose, data dependency, accountable operator, acceptance evidence and recovery action. Keep a separate deferred list with the reason each flow is waiting.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Stage one: establish the sending boundary</h2>
    <p>Inventory existing senders before building. Include Shopify notifications, capture apps, agency automations and subscription messages. Name the system responsible for each message purpose. Keep working order notifications under their existing owner unless replacing them is explicitly in scope. Confirm sender authentication using the selected provider’s instructions and test the actual reply address. Give implementers only the access their tasks require; assign an internal owner who can disable the release without waiting for the agency.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Stage two: turn the brief into a testable contract</h2>
    <p>Write entry and dispatch rules separately. A customer can qualify at signup and become ineligible before a delayed message. Define what happens after a purchase, unsubscribe, refund, stock change or support escalation. Specify which other flows can coexist and which must yield. For each branch record the source field, expected values, treatment of missing data and delay before the builder sees updates. A segment label alone is not a sufficient specification.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Stage three: test one change before expanding scope</h2>
    <p>Proposed acceptance cases: a new subscriber who buys while waiting; a returning buyer with an active cart; an opted-out profile; an order cancellation arriving after scheduling; and an unavailable product. Use controlled accounts and permitted test events. Record expected and actual dispatch decisions, timestamps, rendered links, offers and mobile presentation. A preview proves rendering only. It does not prove entry, exclusion or delayed-send behavior. If a long delay cannot be tested safely, validate it in an isolated copy and record that production timing remains untested.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Stage four: release with an owner watching</h2>
    <p>Select a limited eligible audience that the operator can inspect and define the observation window around the flow’s delay and purchase cycle. Keep campaign volume and unrelated automation changes stable where feasible. Review queue entries, actual sends, errors, opt-outs and support reports. Define stop conditions before enabling: duplicate dispatch, failed exclusion or broken offer should have a named response. Do not use an arbitrary open-rate target to waive functional failures. Record who may pause the flow and who authorizes re-entry.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Stage five: hand over operation</h2>
    <p>The handoff contains the live version, release time, test record, field definitions, priority rules, offer dependencies, dashboard definitions and rollback instructions. Agree a review cadence based on risk and event frequency. Review again after integration, catalog, subscription or consent changes. A new operator should be able to explain why a sampled customer received a message and how to stop the next one. Keep deferred flows visible rather than quietly enabling templates after sign-off.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Two proposed scope decisions</h2>
    <p>A tea store with delivery questions might release brewing education before building a reorder reminder; the reorder project waits for usable purchase history. A label approaching a fixed release date might ship only a consented announcement with tested stock links, leaving browse recovery out of scope. These are planning examples, not observed merchant outcomes. A fixed calendar should reduce scope rather than remove suppression tests.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify ledger-card">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Platform selection and integration evidence</h2>
    <p>Apply the same release contract to Sequenzy or any shortlisted sender. Require a demonstration of the actual event, delay, exit and export path; a generated workflow draft still needs operator review. Shopify’s Order reference describes purchase, fulfillment and refund data; the Customer reference describes purchase history and marketing preferences. Those API objects establish possible source data, not proof that a particular email app imports it. Record connector coverage and latency separately.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><p>Source scope: <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Order">Shopify Order reference</a> and <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Customer">Customer reference</a>, checked November 24, 2026. Supplier capabilities and legal conclusions are outside this source scope.</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><p>Related: <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Shopify Email Migration</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Shopify Email Pricing Models</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/replenishment-tools">Shopify Replenishment Tools</a></p></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
